  "catalog_sha256": "da979b05a68af864cb0dcf9ee6a81c010c7e66a57978ad286c7a2e005fc69a67",
  "df_floor": 100,
  "rationale": "A document-frequency floor alone does not keep the target's own catalog text out of the authoring prompt for Attribute.FEATURE. Measured on the frozen 50,000-product catalog: 136,232 distinct feature values, of which 11,522 clear DF>=2 and 90 clear DF>=100, and the high-DF survivors are verbatim catalog boilerplate -- \"imported\" on 13,832 products, \"machine wash\" on 8,899, \"rubber sole\" on 5,616. So any DF floor at or below 100 admits literal spans of the target's own searchable_text into the prompt. The feature bucket cannot simply be dropped instead: feature is 50.5% of all control-card constraints. This table is therefore the mechanism that keeps \"no catalog text reaches the authoring prompt\" a data-flow property a test can assert, rather than a prompt-discipline instruction a future author could quietly violate. Every admitted feature gist pair is an (attribute, token) drawn from this table, never the catalog string, and neither half of the pair may appear as a substring of its own source value after normalization -- an echoing abstraction is not an abstraction.",
  "schema_version": 1
}
